{#- Gate-0 sentences that appear in BOTH the per-stage block and the fused preamble.

    Macros rather than `{% include %}`: an include carries its file's trailing newline
    (the env sets keep_trailing_newline), which cannot appear mid-paragraph. A macro
    body with `{%- -%}` trimming inserts exactly the sentence.

    ADR-016 (validator-3 M3) is the reason this file exists at all. The guard paragraph
    has three variants — base, base + standalone, base + extra note — so a naive
    intersection hoist leaves the tails behind as orphan fragments continuing a sentence
    that now lives 2,000 lines upstream. Hoisting the BASE and re-rendering each tail as
    its own self-contained sentence is what keeps them readable in place. -#}
{#- `where` exists because "below" is true beside the fence and false in a preamble
    that sits ~2,000 lines above every fence. ADR-016's position-correctness rule. -#}
{% macro guard_base(where="below") -%}
The shell guard {{ where }} makes the receipt a no-op when `.current-iter` is absent — that file is written only by the autoloop driver at iter start. Standalone runs (no autoloop), no-isolation runs, and post-`/compact` restoration before iter 1 all skip the write naturally. This is by design — Gate 0 only reads receipts written under `iter-N` for N≥1.
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro standalone_note(stage) -%}
In a standalone `/hm:{{ stage }}` the driver has not written `.current-iter`, so the guard's `[ -f ]` test is false and no write fires.
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro skipped_rule() -%}
**`skipped` is never emitted from a stage prompt.** It is reserved for the autoloop driver's auto-retry escape hatch (ADR-005 of PLAN-loop-mid-stop-and-review-skip), so each stage's verdict list below offers only `pass` and `fail`.
{%- endmacro %}
